The expression $m_{
u} ightarrow \infty$ denotes the limit where the mass of an active neutrino, $m_{
u}$, approaches infinity. This is a purely theoretical construct, as all experimental evidence from neutrino oscillation experiments points to very small, but non-zero, masses for at least two neutrino mass eigenstates. Within the framework of the Type-I Seesaw Mechanism, where the light neutrino mass is given by $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$, this limit is deeply problematic. For a finite Dirac mass term $m_D$, an infinite $m_{
u}$ would imply a vanishing right-handed neutrino mass ($M_R ightarrow 0$), which contradicts the foundational assumption of the seesaw mechanism (a very heavy $M_R$). Therefore, this limit signifies a breakdown of the seesaw approximation or an unphysical parameter choice.

infinite-neutrino-mass-limit-3f3a21c7.limits_and_boundary:
This limit is a boundary condition itself. In the context of the seesaw formula $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$, taking the limit $m_{
u} ightarrow \infty$ (for a fixed Dirac mass $m_D$) forces the right-handed neutrino mass to zero, i.e., $M_R ightarrow 0$. This is the opposite of the canonical seesaw limit where $M_R ightarrow \infty$ leads to a small, physically observed $m_{
u}$. Thus, the $m_{
u} ightarrow \infty$ limit represents a singular, unphysical point in the parameter space of the theory.
